A||N||3||johnmac||Caliper & Piston Tolerance||||1||
Z||000000||johnmac||08-20-2000||09:17 PM||||What is the proper tolerance between the caliper cylinder walls and the piston. I am sending my former frozen calipers to machine shop for work.<P>Any ideals?<P>John MacHaffie<BR>86 GT - Restoration almost done.||||reg||1
Z||000001||theogre||08-20-2000||10:41 PM||||If the pistons are damaged you have to replace them. You can't machine them. The piston is a calibrated precision part.<P>The caliper bore needs only to be cleaned and honed to remove scale. DO NOT bore it out. DO NOT oversize the groove where the seal lives. The caliper bore is calibrated but does not require the polish the pistons do.<P>I strongly suggest you get a book about brakes. Quickly.<BR><p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 08-20-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||johnmac||08-21-2000||02:19 AM||||Thanks for the reply.<P>One damaged piston will be replaced with a new one.<P>There is no evidence of wear on the cylinder walls and piston. I just knew it is pretty hard to move the piston back in. <P>I should just clean them, put in new rubber seals and o-rings and put the piston back in.<BR>Is this correct.<P>Brake Book - Hey, I still on page 3 of my Fiero Restoration book - - were it says <BR>"YOU DID WHAT!"<P>Thanks,<P>John<P>||||reg||1
Z||000003||theogre||08-21-2000||04:09 PM||||On the front calipers that's about it. make sure the grooves the seals go in are all cleaned too. be careful not to score the grooves or the seals may leak.<P>Before pushing the pistons in coat the seal O ring with brake fluid. That will lube it a bit and make the piston start in easier.<P>Don't even mess with the rears. There's a whole raft of things that can screw them up. Even those of us that do allot of this work avoid them if we can. Costs more to buy rebuilt ones but less likely to have problems. Plus those have a warrantee if there are problems.||||reg||1
